\chapter{Primitive Parsers and Basic
Combinators}\label{ch24-primitive-parsers-and-basic-combinators}

Chapter 23 introduced the combinator-parser core of MuDSL: the
\texttt{ParsecInput} cursor, the
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
wrapper, and the
\texttt{ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
value type that carries either a parsed value or a \texttt{ParseError}.
This chapter opens up the box of primitive parsers and combinators that
ship in the \texttt{dsl} namespace and shows how they compose into
recognisers for non-trivial grammars. The treatment is deliberately
concrete. Every code fragment uses the real
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}} API and compiles against a
C++20 toolchain with \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} included.

The combinators described here form the substrate on which the heavier
machinery of Chapter 25 (diagnostics, semantic actions,
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} plumbing) and Chapter 27 (the PEG
definition grammar) is built. A reader who works through the worked
examples will already be able to write small recursive-descent
recognisers for identifiers, numbers, and \texttt{key=value} pairs
before semantic actions are introduced. Where the chapter touches on the
run pipeline or production handlers, it forward-references Chapter 25
rather than duplicating its material.

\section{The Parser Abstraction in
Brief}\label{ch24-the-parser-abstraction-in-brief}

A \texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
a wrapper around
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textless{}\allowbreak{}ExpectedResult\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}ParsecInput\&)\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Constructing one is a matter of handing a callable to
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parser}, which deduces \texttt{T}
from the callable's return type. The callable receives the mutable
\texttt{ParsecInput} by reference, advances \texttt{in.\allowbreak{}pos}
as it consumes input, and returns either a value or a failure. This is
the only contract a primitive parser must honour.

The \texttt{ParsecInput} struct exposes three primitives used throughout
this chapter: \texttt{peek(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns the
character under the cursor or \texttt{'\textbackslash{}0'} at
end-of-file, \texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns the
character under the cursor and advances, and
\texttt{eof(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} reports whether the cursor has
reached the end. A parser that matches one character typically calls
\texttt{peek(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} to test, then
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} to accept. The cursor is
the sole carrier of mutable state, which makes backtracking trivial:
restoring \texttt{in.\allowbreak{}pos} to a saved value undoes every
side effect of a speculative parse.

Failures carry a \texttt{ParseFailureKind\allowbreak{}} of either
\texttt{Soft} or \texttt{Committed}. The distinction governs how the
choice combinator \texttt{|} behaves, and it is examined in detail later
in this chapter. For now it suffices to know that a \texttt{Soft}
failure is a speculative miss from which the combinator core may
backtrack, while a \texttt{Committed} failure signals that input was
consumed and the alternative branch should not be tried. The primitives
presented in the next section all fail softly.

\section{Primitive: Matching a Single
Character}\label{ch24-primitive-matching-a-single-character}

The two single-character primitives are
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ch} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}satisfy}. The first matches one
specific character; the second matches one character that satisfies a
predicate. Between them they cover every common case of single-token
recognition, including the character classes that other libraries ship
as named parsers.

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ch(\allowbreak{}c)\allowbreak{}}
returns a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that succeeds when the character under the cursor equals \texttt{c},
consuming it, and fails softly otherwise. Its definition is short enough
to quote verbatim.

\begin{code}
inline Parser<char>
ch (char c)
{
  return labeled (
      parser ([c] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<char> {
        if (in.peek () == c)
          return in.consume ();
        return fail_expected<char> (in, std::string (1, c));
      }),
      std::string (1, c));
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{labeled} wrapper, examined later in this chapter, attaches
the character itself as the ``expected'' label so that diagnostic
messages read naturally. A
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is the smallest useful building block in the library; almost every
grammar in the examples begins by combining \texttt{ch} with itself or
with \texttt{satisfy}.

The second primitive,
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}satisfy(\allowbreak{}pred,\allowbreak{} label)\allowbreak{}},
generalises \texttt{ch} to an arbitrary predicate on the peeked
character. It is the canonical way to express a character class. Its
definition is:

\begin{code}
inline Parser<char>
satisfy (std::function<bool (char)> pred, std::string label)
{
  return labeled (
      parser ([pred = std::move (pred), label] (
                  ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<char> {
        if (!in.eof () && pred (in.peek ()))
          return in.consume ();
        return fail_expected<char> (in, label);
      }),
      std::move (label));
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{label} argument is the string reported in errors when the
predicate fails. Passing \texttt{"digit"}, \texttt{"letter"}, or
\texttt{"whitespace"} makes diagnostics self-documenting. The predicate
is stored by value in the captured lambda, so a \texttt{satisfy}-based
parser is copyable and storable in a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

\section{Character Classes as
Combinators}\label{ch24-character-classes-as-combinators}

MuDSL does not ship named \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}digit}
or \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}letter} parsers. The intended
idiom, demonstrated in
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}05-parser-basics\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}cpp}
and
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}07-parser-option\allowbreak{}al-repeat-choice\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}cpp},
is to define them at the call site with \texttt{satisfy}. This keeps the
primitive set small and the label string under the grammar author's
control.

\begin{code}
auto digit  = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c >= '0' && c <= '9'; }, "digit");
auto letter = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c >= 'a' && c <= 'z'; }, "letter");
auto upper  = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z'; }, "upper");
auto alnum  = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return std::isalnum(static_cast<unsigned char>(c)); }, "alnum");
auto ws     = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n'; }, "whitespace");
\end{code}

Each of these is a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
They compose with the operators introduced later in exactly the same way
that \texttt{ch} does. The naming is purely local: \texttt{digit} is a
variable, not a member of the \texttt{dsl} namespace, which is why the
examples always spell it in lower case at point of use.

The any-character parser sometimes called \texttt{item} in other
combinator libraries is expressed by passing a predicate that always
returns \texttt{true}. Because \texttt{satisfy} already guards against
end-of-input, this yields a parser that consumes exactly one character
when one is available and fails softly at EOF.

\begin{code}
auto item = dsl::satisfy([](char){ return true; }, "any character");
\end{code}

This idiom is rarely needed in practice --- grammars usually
discriminate on character class --- but it is the right tool when a
grammar must consume and echo an arbitrary byte, for instance inside a
fallback rule.

\section{Matching a Literal String}\label{ch24-matching-a-literal-string}

There is no built-in
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}} primitive.
A literal-string recogniser is assembled from \texttt{ch} and the
sequence and repetition combinators introduced below, or --- more
compactly --- from \texttt{satisfy} plus a manual equality test. A
typical definition folds a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view} into a
parser that consumes the whole run or fails softly without consuming
anything.

\begin{code}
dsl::Parser<std::string> string_(std::string_view s) {
  return dsl::parser([s](dsl::ParsecInput& in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string> {
    for (char c : s) {
      if (in.peek() != c)
        return dsl::fail_expected<std::string>(in, std::string(s));
      in.consume();
    }
    return std::string(s);
  });
}
\end{code}

This helper consumes input only when the full literal matches; on a
mismatch it restores nothing because the loop body returns before
consuming. A more conservative variant would snapshot
\texttt{in.\allowbreak{}pos} at entry and restore it on failure, so that
a partial match leaves the cursor untouched. The sequence combinator
\texttt{\&} already provides that guarantee when a literal is built by
folding \texttt{ch} over the characters of the string, which is the
approach used in the worked examples later in this chapter.

The
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
return type is deliberate: a string matched character-by-character via
\texttt{ch} would otherwise accumulate as a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}char,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}char,\allowbreak{} .\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
nesting under \texttt{\&}, which is awkward to use. Returning a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} directly from a single
primitive keeps the value type flat.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Repetition: The \texttt{*}
Operator}{Repetition: The  Operator}}\label{ch24-repetition-the-operator}

Repetition of zero or more matches is expressed by the unary
\texttt{operator*}. Applying \texttt{*p} yields a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that runs \texttt{p} repeatedly until it fails, collecting each
successful value into a vector. The combinator always succeeds, even
when \texttt{p} matches zero times --- the resulting vector is simply
empty.

The definition is worth quoting in full, because its control flow
encodes the library's policy on committed failures and non-progress.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
Parser<std::vector<T>>
operator* (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser (
      [p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>>
        {
          std::vector<T> out;
          ParseError best_err{};
          while (true)
            {
              std::size_t save = in.pos;
              auto r = p (in);
              if (!r)
                {
                  merge_error (best_err, r.error);
                  if (r.error.kind == ParseFailureKind::Committed)
                    return ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>>::failure (
                        best_err.pos, best_err.kind, best_err.expected);
                  in.pos = save;
                  break;
                }
              out.push_back (*r);
            }
          return out;
        });
}
\end{code}

Two properties deserve emphasis. First, on a soft failure the cursor is
restored to \texttt{save} before the loop breaks, so a trailing partial
match leaves no residue. Second, a committed failure aborts the whole
repetition and propagates the committed status; this is how a sequence
inside the repeated parser can short-circuit the loop. The accumulated
\texttt{best\_\allowbreak{}err} is merged so that the deepest
speculative failure is the one reported.

A direct consequence of the loop structure is that a parser \texttt{p}
which succeeds without consuming input will loop forever. The combinator
performs no progress check; the grammar author is responsible for
ensuring that every operand of \texttt{*} consumes at least one
character on success. This pitfall is revisited at the end of the
chapter.

\section{One-or-More Repetition}\label{ch24-one-or-more-repetition}

The core combinators expose only the Kleene star (\texttt{*p}, zero or
more). The plus repetition --- one or more matches --- is expressed as a
sequence of one mandatory match followed by zero or more optional
matches. Because \texttt{\&} combines two results into a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair}, the idiomatic
\texttt{many1} flattens the pair into a single vector.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
dsl::Parser<std::vector<T>> many1(const dsl::Parser<T>& p) {
  return dsl::parser([p](dsl::ParsecInput& in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>> {
    auto first = p(in);
    if (!first)
      return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>>::failure(
          first.error.pos, first.error.kind, first.error.expected);
    std::vector<T> out;
    out.push_back(*first);
    auto rest = *p;
    auto r = rest(in);
    if (!r)
      return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>>::failure(
          r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
    for (auto& v : *r) out.push_back(std::move(v));
    return out;
  });
}
\end{code}

With this helper in scope,
\texttt{many1(\allowbreak{}digit)\allowbreak{}} parses \texttt{42} into
a vector of two characters and fails on an empty input. The failure is
soft when \texttt{digit} fails softly, which means a \texttt{many1}
operand can be used as the first alternative in a \texttt{|} choice
without committing the input.

The PEG layer introduced in Chapter 27 ships its own
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}many} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}many1} with
identical semantics but additional channel bookkeeping; readers who
reach for repetition inside a PEG definition should prefer those. This
chapter's \texttt{many1} is the appropriate tool when working directly
with the core combinators.

\section{Optional Parsing}\label{ch24-optional-parsing}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}}
wraps a parser so that a soft failure is reported as success with an
empty
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
A committed failure propagates unchanged, preserving the contract that
committed input cannot be backtracked. The return type is
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
Parser<std::optional<T>>
optional (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser (
      [p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<std::optional<T>>
        {
          auto save = in.pos;
          auto r = p (in);
          if (!r)
            {
              if (r.error.kind == ParseFailureKind::Committed)
                return ExpectedResult<std::optional<T>>::failure (
                    r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
              in.pos = save;
              return std::optional<T>{};
            }
          return std::optional<T>{ *r };
        });
}
\end{code}

The snapshot-and-restore on the soft-failure path means
\texttt{optional} never leaves the cursor advanced when it returns an
empty optional. This is essential for idioms like an optional sign
character followed by digits: if the sign is absent, the digit parser
must see the input from the original position.

\begin{code}
auto sign = dsl::optional(dsl::ch('-'));
auto digit = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c >= '0' && c <= '9'; }, "digit");
auto num = sign & *digit;
\end{code}

The type of \texttt{num} is
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The sign, when present, is recovered by dereferencing the first element
of the pair. Example 07 in the distribution uses exactly this shape to
parse \texttt{-42}.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Sequencing Parsers: The \texttt{\&}
Operator}{Sequencing Parsers: The  Operator}}\label{ch24-sequencing-parsers-the-operator}

The binary \texttt{operator\&} expresses sequencing. The expression
\texttt{a \& b} produces a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}A,\allowbreak{} B\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
that runs \texttt{a}, then --- on success --- runs \texttt{b} against
the advanced cursor, and returns the two results as a pair. Failure of
either operand aborts the sequence.

\begin{code}
template <typename A, typename B>
Parser<std::pair<A, B>>
operator& (const Parser<A> &a, const Parser<B> &b)
{
  return parser (
      [a, b] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<std::pair<A, B>>
        {
          auto save = in.pos;
          auto ra = a (in);
          if (!ra)
            return ExpectedResult<std::pair<A, B>>::failure (
                ra.error.pos, ra.error.kind, ra.error.expected);
          auto rb = b (in);
          if (!rb)
            {
              bool consumed = in.pos != save;
              in.pos = save;
              ParseFailureKind kind = rb.error.kind;
              if (consumed)
                kind = ParseFailureKind::Committed;
              return ExpectedResult<std::pair<A, B>>::failure (
                  rb.error.pos, kind, rb.error.expected);
            }
          return std::make_pair (*ra, *rb);
        });
}
\end{code}

Two details shape the practical use of \texttt{\&}. First, when
\texttt{b} fails after \texttt{a} consumed input, the sequence promotes
the failure to \texttt{Committed} before returning it; this prevents a
surrounding \texttt{|} from silently backtracking over the consumed
prefix. Second, the cursor is always restored to \texttt{save} on
failure, so a failed sequence leaves no residue.

Because \texttt{\&} nests to the right, chaining three parsers produces
a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}A,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}B,\allowbreak{} C\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Grammars that need a flat value typically post-process the pair with a
\texttt{map}-style transform or build a custom parser that returns the
desired aggregate. The AST-construction combinators of Chapter 11 are
the recommended tool when the goal is a tree rather than a tuple.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Ordered Choice: The \texttt{|}
Operator}{Ordered Choice: The  Operator}}\label{ch24-ordered-choice-the-operator}

The binary \texttt{operator|} expresses ordered choice. The expression
\texttt{a | b} runs \texttt{a}; if \texttt{a} succeeds, its result is
returned. If \texttt{a} fails softly, the cursor is restored and
\texttt{b} is tried from the same position. If \texttt{a} fails
committed, the failure is returned without trying \texttt{b}. The two
operands must have the same result type \texttt{T}.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
Parser<T>
operator| (const Parser<T> &a, const Parser<T> &b)
{
  return parser (
      [a, b] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<T>
        {
          auto save = in.pos;
          auto ra = a (in);
          if (ra)
            return ra;
          if (ra.error.kind == ParseFailureKind::Committed)
            return ra;
          in.pos = save;
          auto rb = b (in);
          if (!rb)
            merge_error (rb.error, ra.error);
          return rb;
        });
}
\end{code}

Choice in MuDSL is ordered, not longest-match. The first operand that
succeeds wins, regardless of how much input it consumes. This is the PEG
semantic: a grammar \texttt{a | b} is read as ``attempt \texttt{a}; if
it fails, attempt \texttt{b}''. A consequence is that the order of
alternatives matters. Placing a more general alternative before a more
specific one will shadow the specific one permanently.

When both branches fail, \texttt{merge\_\allowbreak{}error} combines the
two errors, preferring the one that made the furthest progress and
unioning their \texttt{expected} labels. This is what produces the
multi-label ``expected digit or letter'' style of diagnostic. The merge
rules are examined in Chapter 25; for this chapter it is enough to know
that the deeper failure wins.

\section{Backtracking and the Soft/Committed
Distinction}\label{ch24-backtracking-and-the-softcommitted-distinction}

The choice and optional combinators both restore
\texttt{in.\allowbreak{}pos} on a soft failure. This is the mechanism of
backtracking: a speculative parse is undone by resetting the cursor to a
saved value, and the next alternative begins from the same position. No
other state needs to be unwound, because \texttt{ParsecInput} carries
only the cursor and the immutable source view.

A committed failure is the combinator core's way of signalling that
backtracking would be incorrect. The sequence combinator \texttt{\&}
commits when its first operand consumed input and its second operand
then failed; the repetition combinator \texttt{*} propagates a committed
failure from its body; \texttt{optional} propagates a committed failure
rather than converting it to an empty optional. The effect is that once
a parser has unambiguously committed to a branch, later alternatives in
a \texttt{|} are not tried.

This is the classic parsec-style committed-choice discipline. It avoids
the incidental backtracking that plagues naive longest-match parsers,
while still allowing speculative alternatives at points where the
grammar is genuinely ambiguous. Chapter 23 covers the underlying
\texttt{ParseFailureKind\allowbreak{}} enum in more detail; Chapter 25
explains how diagnostic labels surface in
\texttt{ParseError:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}expected}.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Relabelling and Softening: \texttt{labeled} and
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse}}{Relabelling and Softening:  and }}\label{ch24-relabelling-and-softening-labeled-and-try_parse}

Two small adapters round out the primitive set.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}labeled(\allowbreak{}p,\allowbreak{} label)\allowbreak{}}
runs \texttt{p} unchanged on success but overwrites the
\texttt{expected} list of a failure with the single label
\texttt{label}. It is how \texttt{ch} and \texttt{satisfy} attach their
friendly diagnostic strings. Grammars can use it to name a sub-rule:
\texttt{labeled(\allowbreak{}many1(\allowbreak{}digit)\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} "integer")\allowbreak{}}
will report \texttt{expected integer\allowbreak{}} on failure rather
than the default \texttt{expected digit}.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
Parser<T>
labeled (const Parser<T> &p, std::string expected)
{
  return parser ([p, expected = std::move (expected)] (
                     ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<T> {
    auto r = p (in);
    if (!r)
      r.error.expected = { expected };
    return r;
  });
}
\end{code}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}try\_\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}p)\allowbreak{}}
is the adapter that demotes a committed failure to a soft one. It
snapshots the cursor, runs \texttt{p}, and on any failure restores the
cursor and rewrites the failure's kind to \texttt{Soft}. This is the
escape hatch when a parser that would otherwise commit should instead be
made speculative --- for example, when wrapping a sequence that is the
first operand of a \texttt{|} and the grammar author wants the second
operand to be tried even after partial consumption.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
Parser<T>
try_parse (const Parser<T> &p)
{
  return parser ([p] (ParsecInput &in) -> ExpectedResult<T> {
    auto save = in.pos;
    auto r = p (in);
    if (!r)
      {
        in.pos = save;
        r.error.kind = ParseFailureKind::Soft;
      }
    return r;
  });
}
\end{code}

Between \texttt{labeled} and \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse}, a grammar
author can control the two things that matter for diagnostics and
backtracking: what a rule is called, and whether a failure blocks
subsequent alternatives. The combinators \texttt{\&}, \texttt{|},
\texttt{*}, and \texttt{optional} are sufficient for everything else.

\section{Running a Parser}\label{ch24-running-a-parser}

\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}run\_\allowbreak{}parser(\allowbreak{}p,\allowbreak{} source)\allowbreak{}}
is the entry point that turns a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} into
a result against a string. It constructs a \texttt{ParsecInput} over the
supplied
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view},
invokes the parser, and packages the outcome in a
\texttt{ParseOutcome\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
--- a struct holding an
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
value and a \texttt{ParseError}.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
ParseOutcome<T>
run_parser (const Parser<T> &p, std::string_view source)
{
  ParsecInput in{ source, 0 };
  auto r = p (in);
  if (!r)
    return { std::nullopt, r.error };
  if (!in.eof ())
    {
      ParseError trailing{ in.pos, ParseFailureKind::Committed,
                           { "<end-of-input>" } };
      return { std::nullopt, trailing };
    }
  return { std::move (r.value), {} };
}
\end{code}

A successful parse must consume the entire input; a trailing suffix
produces a committed failure with the label
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}end-of-input\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
This is stricter than invoking the parser directly against a
\texttt{ParsecInput}, which would happily return a value and leave the
cursor mid-stream. The strictness is deliberate: a top-level grammar
that does not reach end-of-input almost certainly has a hole that should
be reported.

The full run pipeline --- including source loading from files via
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}load\_\allowbreak{}parse\_\allowbreak{}input\_\allowbreak{}file},
line/column resolution for diagnostics, and integration with semantic
actions --- is the subject of Chapter 25. The minimal
\texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} shown here is all that the worked
examples in this chapter require.

\section{Worked Example: A Natural
Number}\label{ch24-worked-example-a-natural-number}

Combining the primitives yields a recogniser for a natural number in a
few lines. The grammar is one or more digits, flattened into a vector of
characters; a production layer (Chapter 25) would convert the vector
into an integer.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto digit = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c >= '0' && c <= '9'; }, "digit");
  auto nat   = *digit;                       // Parser<std::vector<char>>
  auto out   = dsl::run_parser(nat, "4096");
  if (out.value)
    std::cout << "digits: " << out.value->size() << "\n";
  else
    std::cout << "failed at " << out.error.pos << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The Kleene star accepts the empty string, so \texttt{nat} succeeds on
\texttt{""} with an empty vector. When the grammar requires at least one
digit, the \texttt{many1} helper introduced earlier is the right tool.
Note that \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} rejects trailing input:
parsing \texttt{"4096x"} fails at position 4 with the
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}end-of-input\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
label, because the parser stops at \texttt{6} and leaves \texttt{x}
unconsumed.

\section{Worked Example: An
Identifier}\label{ch24-worked-example-an-identifier}

The classic identifier rule
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*} exercises
\texttt{satisfy}, choice, and repetition together. The first character
is a letter or underscore; the tail is zero or more letters,
underscores, or digits.

\begin{code}
auto id_first = dsl::satisfy(
    [](char c){ return std::isalpha(static_cast<unsigned char>(c)) || c == '_'; },
    "identifier-start");
auto id_rest = dsl::satisfy(
    [](char c){ return std::isalnum(static_cast<unsigned char>(c)) || c == '_'; },
    "identifier-continue");
auto identifier = id_first & *id_rest;   // Parser<std::pair<char, std::vector<char>>>
\end{code}

The result type is a pair of the head character and a vector of tail
characters. A production handler can stitch them into a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} in one line; for ad-hoc
use, iterating the pair is straightforward. Because
\texttt{id\_\allowbreak{}first} fails softly on a non-matching
character, \texttt{identifier} can be used as one alternative in a
larger choice without committing the cursor.

\begin{code}
auto out = dsl::run_parser(identifier, "foo_bar99");
if (out.value) {
  char head = out.value->first;
  std::string tail(out.value->second.begin(), out.value->second.end());
  std::cout << head << tail << "\n";      // prints foo_bar99
}
\end{code}

\section{Worked Example: A Quoted
String}\label{ch24-worked-example-a-quoted-string}

A double-quoted string pairs a literal delimiter with a repetition of
any character that is not the closing quote. The
\texttt{string\_\allowbreak{}} helper is unnecessary for the quotes
themselves; \texttt{ch} is sufficient.

\begin{code}
auto quote    = dsl::ch('"');
auto not_quote = dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c != '"'; }, "string-char");
auto quoted   = quote & *not_quote & quote;
\end{code}

The type of \texttt{quoted} is
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}char,\allowbreak{} std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{} char\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The payload is the middle element of the nested pair; the two quote
characters are discarded by any downstream production. Note that
\texttt{*not\_\allowbreak{}quote} accepts the empty string, so
\texttt{""} parses as an empty vector, which is the desired behaviour.

A subtlety: \texttt{not\_\allowbreak{}quote} does not exclude newlines
or backslash escapes. A real string grammar would add an escape
alternative using \texttt{|}, for instance
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}'\textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}' \& item)\allowbreak{} | not\_\allowbreak{}quote},
to handle \texttt{\textbackslash{}"} and
\texttt{\textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}}. The \texttt{item} idiom
defined earlier in the chapter consumes the character following the
backslash without discrimination.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Worked Example:
\texttt{key=value}}{Worked Example: }}\label{ch24-worked-example-keyvalue}

A \texttt{key=value} record combines an identifier, a literal
\texttt{=}, and a value. Sequencing with \texttt{\&} builds the shape
directly.

\begin{code}
auto key   = identifier;                       // from the previous example
auto eq    = dsl::ch('=');
auto value = *dsl::satisfy([](char c){ return c != ';' && c != '\n'; }, "value-char");
auto record = key & eq & value;
\end{code}

The \texttt{record} parser succeeds on \texttt{foo=bar} and fails on
\texttt{foo=}, because \texttt{value} accepts the empty tail but
\texttt{eq} requires the \texttt{=}. Trailing whitespace and semicolons
are rejected by \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser}'s end-of-input check,
which is usually the desired behaviour for a line-oriented record
grammar.

\section{Worked Example: A Comma-Separated
List}\label{ch24-worked-example-a-comma-separated-list}

Comma-separated lists are the canonical application of the \texttt{*}
operator over a sequence. The grammar is: one element, followed by zero
or more \texttt{(\allowbreak{}comma element)\allowbreak{}} pairs. The
element parser is a parameter, so the list combinator is generic.

\begin{code}
template <typename T>
dsl::Parser<std::vector<T>> sep_by1(const dsl::Parser<T>& elem, char sep) {
  auto comma_elem = dsl::ch(sep) & elem;
  return dsl::parser(
      [elem, comma_elem](dsl::ParsecInput& in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>> {
        auto first = elem(in);
        if (!first)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::vector<T>>::failure(
              first.error.pos, first.error.kind, first.error.expected);
        std::vector<T> out;
        out.push_back(*first);
        auto rest = *comma_elem;
        auto r = rest(in);
        if (r) for (auto& p : *r) out.push_back(std::move(p.second));
        return out;
      });
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{comma\_\allowbreak{}elem} parser has type
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}char,\allowbreak{} T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
the second element of each pair is the value to keep. Applying
\texttt{*} to it yields a vector of pairs, from which the list
combinator extracts the \texttt{.\allowbreak{}second} of each. Used as
\texttt{sep\_\allowbreak{}by1(\allowbreak{}digit,\allowbreak{} ',\allowbreak{}')\allowbreak{}},
this parses \texttt{1,\allowbreak{}2,\allowbreak{}3} into a vector of
three characters.

A \texttt{sep\_\allowbreak{}by} variant that accepts the empty list is
built by wrapping \texttt{sep\_\allowbreak{}by1} in \texttt{optional}
and flattening. Because \texttt{optional} returns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the empty case is recoverable by checking
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value} before dereferencing.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}with\_\allowbreak{}action}
Bridge}{The  Bridge}}\label{ch24-the-parse_with_action-bridge}

The core combinators return plain values. Attaching a semantic action
--- a callback fired on a successful match with the matched span in hand
--- is the role of
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse\_\allowbreak{}with\_\allowbreak{}action}.
It runs a parser, records the begin and end positions, and invokes the
action with a \texttt{ParserStage} describing the matched span.

\begin{code}
template <typename T, typename ActionFn>
auto
parse_with_action (
    const Parser<T> &p, ParsecInput &input,
    const std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> &semantics,
    ActionFn &&action)
    -> std::optional<decltype (action (semantics, ParserStage{}))>
{
  std::size_t begin = input.pos;
  auto r = p (input);
  if (!r)
    return std::nullopt;
  ParserStage stage{ begin, input.pos, input.get_span (begin) };
  return action (semantics, stage);
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{semantics} map is a table of named productions, populated by
the table-pass layer described in Chapter 25. This chapter's grammars do
not need it; \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}with\_\allowbreak{}action} is
mentioned here only because it is the natural bridge from a flat
combinator grammar to the production-handler machinery. A reader who has
built a recogniser with the tools above is already one step away from a
fully semantic-actioned parser.

The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}production\textless{}\allowbreak{}Id\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}fn)\allowbreak{}}
helper, also defined in the same region of the header, registers a
handler keyed by a \texttt{FixedString} identifier. It returns a
\texttt{ProductionHandle\allowbreak{}r\textless{}\allowbreak{}Id,\allowbreak{} Fn\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
for the run pipeline to dispatch on. Production handlers are the topic
of Chapter 25 and are not used directly in this chapter's examples.

\section{Pitfall: Left Recursion}\label{ch24-pitfall-left-recursion}

The combinator core is PEG-like and evaluates operands eagerly by
recursion. A grammar rule that begins with itself ---
\texttt{expr = expr \& ch\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}'+')\allowbreak{} \& term | term}
--- will call itself before consuming any input, and so will recurse
without bound until the C++ stack overflows. The library provides no
left-recursion detection or guard.

The standard remedy is the same as for any recursive-descent parser:
rewrite the rule iteratively.
\texttt{expr = term \& *(\allowbreak{}ch(\allowbreak{}'+')\allowbreak{} \& term)\allowbreak{}}
recognises the same language without left recursion, and is in fact the
form used in the worked examples above. The PEG combinators of Chapter
27 inherit the same restriction; the PEG definition grammar of Chapter
28 provides a separate, non-recursive rule expansion mechanism that does
not suffer from this pitfall.

\section{Pitfall: Non-Progressing
Repetition}\label{ch24-pitfall-non-progressing-repetition}

The \texttt{*} combinator's loop body does not check that the operand
consumed input. A parser that succeeds without advancing the cursor ---
for example,
\texttt{optional(\allowbreak{}ch(\allowbreak{}'x')\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
applied to input that does not start with \texttt{x} --- will match an
empty optional at every iteration, appending nothing to the vector and
never terminating.

The defence is grammatical: ensure that every operand of \texttt{*}
either fails or consumes at least one character. A character-class
parser built with \texttt{satisfy} satisfies this by construction, since
\texttt{satisfy} only succeeds by calling
\texttt{consume(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The danger arises when
wrapping \texttt{optional}, \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}parse}, or a
hand-written parser that returns success on a lookahead miss. When in
doubt, assert progress inside the operand.

\section{Pitfall: Ordered Choice Is Not Longest
Match}\label{ch24-pitfall-ordered-choice-is-not-longest-match}

The \texttt{|} operator returns the first alternative that succeeds,
regardless of how much input it consumes. A grammar
\texttt{keyword | identi\allowbreak{}fier} will work correctly only if
\texttt{keyword} is listed first; placing \texttt{identifier} first
would cause every keyword to be parsed as an identifier and shadow the
keyword alternative permanently.

This is the PEG semantic, and it is the price of the predictable,
backtracking-friendly behaviour described earlier. Where longest-match
is genuinely required --- for instance, in a lexer that must prefer
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} over
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} --- the remedy is to order
alternatives from longest to shortest, or to factor the grammar so that
the longer alternative is a strict refinement of the shorter one.
Chapter 9's compile-time
\texttt{pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
engine is the appropriate tool for lexical longest-match concerns; the
combinator core is intended for structural grammar above the token
layer.

\section{Summary}\label{ch24-summary}

This chapter has presented the primitive parsers and basic combinators
that ship in the \texttt{dsl} namespace. The single-character primitives
are \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ch} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}satisfy}; character classes such
as \texttt{digit}, \texttt{letter}, and the any-character \texttt{item}
are defined at the call site with \texttt{satisfy}. Repetition of zero
or more matches is the unary \texttt{*} operator, which returns a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
one-or-more repetition is built from a mandatory match followed by
\texttt{*}. The \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}
combinator wraps a parser to accept zero or one occurrence, returning
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

Sequencing is \texttt{operator\&}, which returns a
\texttt{Parser\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pair\textless{}\allowbreak{}A,\allowbreak{} B\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and commits the sequence when the first operand has consumed input.
Ordered choice is \texttt{operator|}, which tries the left operand and,
on a soft failure, restores the cursor and tries the right; the choice
is ordered, not longest-match. The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}labeled} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}try\_\allowbreak{}parse} adapters
control diagnostic labels and the soft/committed status of failures.
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}run\_\allowbreak{}parser}
executes a parser against a string and requires that the entire input be
consumed.

The worked examples composed these primitives into a natural-number
recogniser, an identifier recogniser, a quoted-string recogniser, a
\texttt{key=value} record, and a comma-separated list. The chapter
closed with the three pitfalls that account for most combinator-grammar
bugs: left recursion, non-progressing repetition, and the ordered
(non-longest-match) semantics of choice. The next chapter, Chapter 25,
builds on this substrate to add diagnostics, semantic actions, and the
full \texttt{run\_\allowbreak{}parser} pipeline; Chapter 27 and Chapter
28 revisit these combinators in the context of the PEG definition
grammar and the PEG matcher.
